WEBVTT

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:54.450
So... what are you singing, exactly?

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.320
It's Schubert's "Demon King!"

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.410
And why that song?

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.290
This song was written in the 19th century.

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:05.120
The composer died more than fifty years ago.

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.710
Which means...

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.040
We can use it in our show

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.090
without paying any royalties to the composer's estate!

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.310
"Demon King," huh?

00:02:53.310 --> 00:02:57.030
I play Schubert's songs on my Steinway now and then, but...

00:02:57.530 --> 00:02:59.650
When I was in middle school,

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:02.950
I really admired people who could play that piece.

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:04.240
I know what you mean.

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:07.620
My teacher in middle school said it was amazing, too.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.830
I think...

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:15.250
it goes: "My father, my father,
the Demon King is coming," right?

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.720
Sakura, you told us before that you were in the Chorus Club,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:22.010
but I never knew you could sing in German, too.

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:25.430
Did you also sing "Die Moldau" in German?

00:03:25.430 --> 00:03:27.260
That's amazing, Sakura!

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.060
You're bilingual!

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.640
Aw...

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:33.940
I learned "Demon King" in middle school, as well,

00:03:33.940 --> 00:03:37.150
but the Demon King in the song is kind of a weak bad guy, isn't he?

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:38.030
Huh?

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:42.610
All he did was seduce a kid and kill him.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.700
He's like an urban legend, at most.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.990
It does seem a bit small-scale
for the work of a Demon King.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:54.290
He also doesn't meet any of the criteria for desirable qualities in a Demon King.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:56.920
Desirable qualities in a Demon King?

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:01.050
Here's what you look for in a Demon King!

00:04:02.090 --> 00:04:04.090
First, overwhelmingly powerful magic.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.760
Next, the charisma to command underlings.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:10.600
And finally, grand ambition!

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:15.600
If those are the criteria, he'd instantly
fail an interview for the Demon King job.

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.980
Failure as a Demon King?!

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.730
After all, kidnapping a child seems
like a job for an underling, doesn't it?

00:04:21.150 --> 00:04:25.110
Then, maybe the song actually went something like this:

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.060
Nothing...

00:04:56.650 --> 00:05:02.320
My boy, 'tis only the wail of the wind.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.990
He's just a grunt...

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.990
Against a Demon King like that,

00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.290
I could have my casters use
normal melee attacks on him.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.250
I'm not gonna waste my MP on you!

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:16.170
Oof!

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:17.370
But he's a Demon King...

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:20.590
He's so low-level, he wouldn't be able to appear if you used Holy Water.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:23.630
Oh, no! Some holy power is protecting him!

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:24.880
But he's a Demon King!

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.130
I bet insta-kill spells would even work on him.

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:27.760
Death!

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:29.760
But he's a Demon King!

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:33.970
I bet he's some low-level monster who just calls himself the Demon King.

00:05:33.970 --> 00:05:35.850
That sounds about right.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:40.230
Perhaps I should report him to the Fair
Trade Commission for false advertising.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.360
No, the more pertinent matter is:

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:45.690
How can we get him to act like a true Demon King should?

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.700
Let's brainstorm that instead!

00:05:49.780 --> 00:05:52.950
The first criterion was
"overwhelmingly powerful magic," right?

00:05:52.950 --> 00:05:54.830
Oh, I see.

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.750
A Demon King would need a reason to abduct children in person.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:00.460
Yes! That is important!

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:07.800
With these changes in mind,

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.800
perhaps it'd go a bit more like this?

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:26.860
Father, I'm scared!

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.860
I can't believe the demonshave discovered that my boy

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:32.070
was born with the mark of a hero on his right hand!

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:34.490
Father! The Demon King!

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:36.580
What?!

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.460
Greetings, future hero.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.920
You are the dark Demon King, Veribadd!

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.510
Out of respect to the hero's mark you bear,

00:06:53.800 --> 00:06:56.720
I have come to slay you with my own hand.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.980
Now, die in the depths of despair!

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.730
Dark Meteor!

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:12.740
Huh? Game over?!

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:15.280
Well, he's definitely more like a Demon King now,

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:16.910
but doesn't that make the game impossible?!

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:20.080
Well, the story had a bad ending to begin with.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:23.380
This is just the inevitable result
of making the Demon King stronger.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:27.130
Besides, it gives the song a moving finale!

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.220
He comes back to life?!

00:07:40.730 --> 00:07:42.730
Even Kintaro would be satisfied!

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:45.730
This is much more reliable than investing in foreign exchange!

00:07:45.730 --> 00:07:47.820
Gold is a safe investment.

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:50.690
If you want to buy gold, come to Yaezakura Gold!

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:52.950
Yaezakura Gold!

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:02.000
Chronicles of the Going Home Club

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:05.710
Well, now that the commercial's over,

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:10.380
let's get hyped up for our "Reinvent the
Demon King: Before and After" in the B part!

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:19.850
My life's goal is to cultivate my willpower

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.980
and become a strong person.

00:08:24.060 --> 00:08:27.400
You say such wonderful things, Botan.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:29.440
Are you K now?

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.110
What kind of retort was that?

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:35.780
But what about reinventing the Demon King?

00:08:36.070 --> 00:08:40.160
Well, once I heard that sound, I lost the will to do it.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:41.830
"That sound"?

00:08:42.370 --> 00:08:43.660
"Toka-ton-ton":

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:47.000
A mysterious sound that comes from nowhere and

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.130
makes you lose the motivation for what you're doing.

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:52.710
It comes from Dazai Osamu's short story, "Tokatonton."

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:56.550
By the way, it's often used as an excuse by lazy writers.

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:12.860
We second years are currently reading
Natsume Soseki's <i>Kokoro</i> in Modern Lit.

00:09:13.610 --> 00:09:15.740
K is one of the characters.

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:18.120
Have you two read it?

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:21.540
In middle school, I read it for a book report.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:23.250
How about you, Karin?

00:09:24.370 --> 00:09:25.620
<i>Kokoro</i>?

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.500
You've never read it, have you?

00:09:29.500 --> 00:09:33.300
<i>Kokoro</i> is really popular in our class right now.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.050
Since last week, more and more people

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.720
seem to be going out to buy the novel.

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:40.930
It's become quite a movement.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.100
But it's in the textbook.

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.100
People are still buying the novel?

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.150
What's in the textbook is only an extract from the full novel.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:51.190
A lot of students want to read the whole thing.

00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:52.820
So now...

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.610
Our classroom has its own <i>Kokoro</i> collection!

00:09:56.610 --> 00:09:57.860
Wow!

00:09:57.860 --> 00:09:59.570
That's amazing!

00:09:59.780 --> 00:10:03.830
Claire, has this movement reached your class?

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:07.000
It's not to that extent in my class.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:11.670
But my textbook is custom-made, so it contains the full text.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:13.250
That's a pointless customization!

00:10:13.500 --> 00:10:16.010
Since the opportunity presented itself, I tried

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.010
reading it from the start, too, but...

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:19.010
How was it?

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:20.930
The full version felt weird.

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:22.760
Are you critiquing a J-Pop song?!

00:10:22.760 --> 00:10:26.430
But that Sensei character is such a NEET...

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:28.850
The hook was the only good part of it.

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:29.690
Where?!

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:31.230
Where's the hook in a novel?!

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:34.270
Anyway, lately, the people in my class have been quoting

00:10:34.270 --> 00:10:36.570
lines from <i>Kokoro</i> for all situations.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:38.280
Right, Botan?

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:38.820
Yeah.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:41.410
This is the most popular one.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.120
<b>S</b>piritually, you must...

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.450
<b>k</b>eep seeking to improve yourself or you're a...

00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:47.000
<b>b</b>uffoon!

00:10:48.040 --> 00:10:50.000
There it is! SKB!

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.460
You abbreviate it?!

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:52.920
When would you ever use that?!

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:55.710
Um, what does that phrase even mean?

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:59.880
One of the characters in the novel unleashes it on his best friend, K,

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.550
as a killing blow to his heart.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.970
Oh, I see.

00:11:04.970 --> 00:11:07.020
Well, that's not exactly wrong, but...

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:10.640
There was a dead seal at Kinosaki.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:16.320
But there are a lot of good stories in our literature textbooks.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.190
When I was in middle school,

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:21.150
I really liked reading <i>Memories of Youth</i>.

00:11:21.150 --> 00:11:23.200
That's a short story by Herman Hesse, right?

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.120
So that's the kind of guy you are.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:27.080
Uh...

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:32.210
I was like, why are these people getting
so heated over something like bugs?

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:34.630
Huh? She hasn't read it at all!

00:11:35.290 --> 00:11:36.380
Yeah, you're right.

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:40.630
The emperor moth and Ryuji the
praying mantis are like cockroaches.

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:41.880
They're all just bugs.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.050
You people get right to the heart of things...

00:11:44.050 --> 00:11:47.890
Oh yeah... Wasn't there a story about going out to buy apples?

00:11:48.350 --> 00:11:49.560
Apples?

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.140
What kind of story was it?

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:52.270
I think I know.

00:11:52.270 --> 00:11:54.480
A little fox goes out on a snowy day to buy apples.

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:57.480
The little fox went out to buy mittens.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:11:59.400
What happened in it?

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:00.820
Let me see...

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:04.870
I think Takashi-kun takes 1,000 yen with him to go shopping,

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:07.030
and the apples are 80 yen apiece...

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:12.790
Never mind. That was math.

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.330
How did you get those mixed up?!

00:12:14.750 --> 00:12:16.630
Do you use that for everything?!

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.250
The seal laughed and laughed!

00:12:21.010 --> 00:12:23.590
I always loved "Run, Melos!"

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.510
That's by Dazai Osamu, right?

00:12:25.510 --> 00:12:27.680
Melos and Campanella's friendship...

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.180
was truly wonderful!

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:30.600
You're mixing up two different stories!

00:12:31.140 --> 00:12:32.270
That isn't right?

00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:35.060
Melos using the Galactic Railroad would be cheating!

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:38.900
Giovanni, your father will return with a sealskin coat!

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:40.320
It's otter skin!

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:41.690
That's not it.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:43.570
"Run, Melos!" goes like this:

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:46.240
Melos gets enraged!

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:51.410
He decides that he must eliminate the evil tyrant king!

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:57.670
But just then, he hears the "toka-ton-ton."

00:12:57.670 --> 00:12:59.670
Ah, forget this.

00:12:59.670 --> 00:13:01.210
Melos fails!

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.550
There's also "The Moon Over the Mountain."

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:06.930
"The Moon Over the Mountain"?

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:09.180
It's about a minor official who fails to become

00:13:09.180 --> 00:13:11.140
a poet, so he becomes a tiger instead.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.310
Well, that's not entirely wrong, but...

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:16.350
Becomes... a tiger?

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:19.690
Botan! A tiger!

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:21.900
You will become a tiger!

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.490
No, it's not like that at all...

00:13:31.490 --> 00:13:33.790
I'll explain it so you can understand.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:37.250
"The Moon Over the Mountain" goes like this:

00:13:41.630 --> 00:13:44.300
Today was another miserable day.

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.260
Every Sunday night,

00:13:46.260 --> 00:13:48.180
I find myself thinking the same thing.

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:52.560
It's a lazy day off, when nothing happens and nothing changes.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.890
I wish things could stay like this forever.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:13:58.600
But...

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:19.080
Is that you, Atsushi-kun?

00:14:19.080 --> 00:14:20.420
Yes, it's me.

00:14:20.790 --> 00:14:25.590
My overwhelming narcissism has transformed me into a tiger.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:30.430
Come on, smile.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.260
Despite this wretched form I've ended up in,

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:36.680
I still dream of seeing all the narcissistic poems

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:40.350
I've written since middle school published in an anthology,

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:43.440
debuting to rave reviews and a huge print run,

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:47.110
and having all the girls falling at my feet.

00:14:47.570 --> 00:14:48.700
I know!

00:14:48.700 --> 00:14:51.110
Before my soul turns completely into a tiger's,

00:14:51.110 --> 00:14:53.160
please listen to the poems I've written.

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:54.370
They're all masterpieces!

00:14:54.370 --> 00:14:56.370
I just know they'll touch you deeply!

00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:59.540
Way to scare someone off...

00:14:59.540 --> 00:15:04.420
In other words, if a poet does not train
himself mentally, he'll become a tiger, right?

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:09.880
Anyway, funny how we all just have vague recollections of these stories.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:11.970
Because we read all of them in class, I guess.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:14.100
No wonder our memories are so vague.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:16.970
You sound completely unashamed about that!

00:15:17.560 --> 00:15:21.980
By the way, I saw a commercial using the character Kintaro recently...

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.940
Why are you bringing that up?

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:26.320
Well, it made me realize that I don't

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.440
remember the story of Kintaro very well.

00:15:28.940 --> 00:15:30.570
Yeah, that's true...

00:15:30.570 --> 00:15:34.910
Though, I remember Momotaro and Urashima Taro pretty well.

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:37.700
Didn't he sumo-wrestle a bear?

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:40.960
Yeah, I think I sorta remember that happening.

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:44.290
But the way you always picture Kintaro is like <i>that</i>, right?

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:47.460
Yeah, Kintaro with an ax on his shoulder.

00:15:50.720 --> 00:15:51.880
Ax?

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.010
Ax?

00:15:54.390 --> 00:15:56.510
Where did he use an ax, again?

00:15:58.470 --> 00:15:59.980
The part where he kills a bear?

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:02.190
His story isn't that graphic!

00:16:02.480 --> 00:16:05.900
Or no matter how many times he chops the bear, it still has the same face?

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:06.940
You're thinking of Kintaro candy!

00:16:07.650 --> 00:16:09.150
I remember now.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:11.990
Kintaro drops the ax into a lake.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:14.320
Then, a bear rises out of it...

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:16.320
Kintaro.

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:19.830
Did you drop this golden ax or this silver—

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:21.370
That's Aesop!

00:16:21.830 --> 00:16:24.370
Didn't the village chief send Kintaro out

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:26.960
on a quest to hunt bears with his ax?

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:30.460
Kintaro didn't go hunting, and
he never made bear-based equipment!

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.800
But that game is really tough at first, you know?

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:35.720
It's hard to make any progress.

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:37.640
That's because you try to solo them!

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:40.020
Maybe it's more like this!

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:45.440
Being late on my first day after
transferring to a new school is the pits!

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:51.530
What happens next?

00:16:51.780 --> 00:16:53.700
Still in the circle! Still in! Still in!

00:16:53.700 --> 00:16:55.660
Still in! Still in! Still in!

00:17:04.250 --> 00:17:06.330
Whoa! It's a bear!

00:17:06.330 --> 00:17:07.830
He still killed it in the end?!

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:09.210
Hand over your millet dumpling!

00:17:10.000 --> 00:17:12.510
If our memory of Kintaro is this vague,

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:16.300
you think we really remember Momotaro
and Urashima Taro as well as we thought?

00:17:16.590 --> 00:17:18.800
Let's find out, shall we?

00:17:19.350 --> 00:17:21.930
I'm pretty sure Momotaro starts like this...

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:28.020
A long time ago...

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:31.400
in a galaxy far, far away,

00:17:31.400 --> 00:17:33.030
lived an old man and a woman...

00:17:33.030 --> 00:17:34.570
How many episodes are there?!

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:37.950
Are Episodes 1 to 3 about the old man?!

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:42.160
Momotaro had a giant, shaggy-haired monkey,
a shiny friend made of gold,

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:44.160
and a robot with a dwarf inside.

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.160
Together, they went off to fight ogres.

00:17:46.160 --> 00:17:47.960
The only thing you got right was the monkey!

00:17:47.960 --> 00:17:50.460
And if you're gonna parody it that much,

00:17:50.790 --> 00:17:53.710
you might as well show the rest of them!

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:01.010
Well, setting that aside,
the old man goes to the mountain,

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:03.770
and the old woman goes down to the river and finds a peach.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:07.890
Then, she performs a C-section and pulls out a little boy!

00:18:07.890 --> 00:18:11.730
You recounted the situation accurately, but your word choice was bizarre!

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:15.280
Then, Momotaro travels around
the country buying up properties

00:18:15.280 --> 00:18:17.070
to defeat the God of Poverty, right?

00:18:27.370 --> 00:18:28.160
Huh?

00:18:28.160 --> 00:18:30.000
Aren't you going to retort?

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:35.920
Oh... I thought it best to not even touch that one, so I let it slide.

00:18:36.340 --> 00:18:37.590
Take 2!

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:41.050
Now that I think about it,

00:18:41.050 --> 00:18:43.560
the idea of a monkey defeating
an ogre is pretty laughable.

00:18:43.560 --> 00:18:46.270
I wouldn't worry about that part too much.

00:18:46.270 --> 00:18:48.390
No, monkeys are tougher than you think.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:50.600
Monkeys are scary!

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.690
What was Urashima Taro's story about?

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:58.780
Uh... I think it starts with some kids harassing a turtle...

00:18:59.240 --> 00:19:00.200
That's right.

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:05.200
Then, he buys the turtle off the bullies and saves it from them.

00:19:05.830 --> 00:19:08.370
In other words, he solved the problem using money.

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:11.080
Yes, but you make it sound so dirty!

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:12.380
Now, now...

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:16.880
If he hadn't used money to solve the problem, he couldn't have saved the turtle.

00:19:16.880 --> 00:19:18.800
Weren't there any other options?!

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:22.090
And if Urashima Taro hadn't saved the turtle...

00:19:24.260 --> 00:19:26.470
I couldn't even save one little turtle...

00:19:27.100 --> 00:19:29.310
I regret ever being born.

00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:31.640
Taro! Live on!

00:19:31.640 --> 00:19:33.610
Just make a boat out of mud!

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:36.570
Now, what was "Fire-Crackle Mountain" about?

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:40.030
It's about a rabbit who traps a raccoon dog and drowns him.

00:19:40.030 --> 00:19:41.070
What?! Wait...

00:19:41.400 --> 00:19:43.320
What about "The Crab and the Monkey"?

00:19:43.320 --> 00:19:46.580
The crab's crew gang up on a
monkey and torture him to death.

00:19:46.580 --> 00:19:48.200
Wait! Wait!

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:49.830
"The Man Who Made the Dead Trees Blossom"?

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:53.250
He burns a dead dog and scatters
the ashes to make a tree blossom.

00:19:53.250 --> 00:19:54.500
That's true, but...

00:19:54.830 --> 00:19:56.500
Now that I think about it...

00:19:56.500 --> 00:19:59.800
a lot of those old folktales are really violent!

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:00.720
They sure are.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:03.550
It's your memories that are making them violent!

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.060
The crows are calling,

00:20:16.310 --> 00:20:17.520
so it's time to go home.

00:20:40.510 --> 00:20:42.470
Hey, Natsuki!

00:20:42.470 --> 00:20:43.550
Coming!

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:20.230
Huh?!

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.770
No one looked into it?

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:25.740
Didn't you look into it, Natsuki?

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:26.780
Well, no.

00:22:27.320 --> 00:22:29.570
I thought one of you would have done so.

00:22:30.030 --> 00:22:33.200
And I figured it wouldn't really matter if we didn't find out, anyway...

00:22:35.450 --> 00:22:36.700
<b>S</b>piritually, you must...

00:22:36.700 --> 00:22:38.620
<b>k</b>eep seeking to improve yourself or you're a...

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:39.540
<b>b</b>uffoon!

00:22:41.420 --> 00:22:43.420
I'm a failure?!

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:46.670
Karin here.

00:22:46.670 --> 00:22:48.880
Did you know that dugongs have crescent-shaped tails,

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:51.340
and manatees have paddle-shaped tails?

00:22:51.340 --> 00:22:54.220
I forget what "kabaddi" tails are shaped like.

00:22:57.520 --> 00:22:58.730
And two others.
